roid Sans Mono&quot;,&quot;Lucida Console&quot;,Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:11.96px;white-space:pre;background-color:rgb(248,248,248)\"><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&quot;DejaVu Sans Mono&quot;,&quot;Droid Sans Mono&quot;,&quot;Lucida Console&quot;,Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:11.96px;white-space:pre\"><span style=\"color:#000\">theme_tree</span></span><span style=\"color:rgb(104,118,135);font-family:&quot;DejaVu Sans Mono&quot;,&quot;Droid Sans Mono&quot;,&quot;Lucida Console&quot;,Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:11.96px;white-space:pre\"><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span></span><span style=\"color:rgb(104,118,135);font-family:&quot;DejaVu Sans Mono&quot;,&quot;Droid Sans Mono&quot;,&quot;Lucida Console&quot;,Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:11.96px;white-space:pre\"><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>However, when performing my beast analysis I left the evolutionary rate set to 1 and thus must scale my tree accordingly. Is there an easy way to do this? I have tried a couple things with no success. I would rather avoid the mess of converting the beast class to a dataframe and parsing and manipulating the data if there is an easy way to manipulate the fields.</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"font-family:&quot;DejaVu Sans Mono&quot;,&quot;Droid Sans Mono&quot;,&quot;Lucida Console&quot;,Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:11.96px;white-space:pre\"><span style=\"color:#000\">plot</span></span><span style=\"color:rgb(104,118,135);font-family:&quot;DejaVu Sans Mono&quot;,&quot;Droid Sans Mono&quot;,&quot;Lucida Console&quot;,Consolas,Monaco,monospace;font-size:11.96px;white-space:pre\"><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span></span><span style=\"font-family:&quot;DejaVu Sans Mono&